Day 1 → HTML Basics
HTML = Structure of a webpage (skeleton).
Tags: <h1> → headings, <p> → paragraphs, <a> → links, <img> → images.
Attributes add extra info: src, href, alt, title.
Semantic tags (like <header>, <footer>, <article>) make content meaningful for both browsers and humans.
Hierarchy matters: Parent tags wrap child tags.

Day 2 → Lists & Tables
Lists:
Ordered (<ol>) → numbered.
Unordered (<ul>) → bullet points.
Tables:
<table> → wrapper.
<tr> → row.
<th> → header cell (bold, centered by default).
<td> → data cell.
Good for structured info like timetables, meal plans, data.

Day 3 → Forms
Used for user input (login, signup, survey).
Inputs: text, email, password, radio, checkbox.
<select> → dropdown, <textarea> → multi-line text.
Always give labels with for="id" → improves accessibility.
Form actions: method="get" (visible in URL), method="post" (hidden, safer).

Day 4 → CSS Basics
CSS = Styling (makeup of the webpage).
3 Ways to add CSS:
Inline (style="") → quick but messy.
Internal (<style> in <head>) → for single file.
External (.css file linked with <link>) → best practice.
Selectors:
Element selector (p {}) → applies to all <p>.
Class selector (.class {}) → reusable.
ID selector (#id {}) → unique.
Cascading rule: Inline > ID > Class > Element.

Day 5 → Colors, Borders, Boxes
Colors: named (red), hex (#ff0000), rgb (rgb(255,0,0)), rgba (with transparency).
Borders: border: width style color; (e.g., 2px solid black).
Box Model:
content → padding → border → margin.
Always remember: Everything in CSS is a box.

Day 6 → Tables + Styling
border-collapse: collapse; → removes double borders in tables.
Cell padding gives breathing space inside cells.
Styling with classes makes tables look cleaner.
Example: Highlight alternate rows using nth-child().

Day 7 → Backgrounds & Fonts
Background properties:
background-color → set color.
background-image → add images.
background-repeat → repeat or not.
background-size: cover; → fill whole area.
background-attachment: fixed; → parallax effect.
Fonts:
font-family: Arial, sans-serif; → fallback system.
Use Google Fonts for modern typography.
text-shadow adds depth.
text-decoration → underline/none/overline.

Day 8 → Display & Position
Display types:
block → takes full width (e.g., <div>).
inline → sits in a line (e.g., <span>).
inline-block → inline but behaves like block.
none → hides element.
Position properties:
static → default, stays in normal flow.
relative → moves relative to itself.
absolute → placed relative to nearest positioned parent.
fixed → sticks to viewport (e.g., navbars).
sticky → acts like relative but sticks when scrolling.
Flexbox vs Grid:
Flexbox → Best for 1D layouts (row or column). Example: aligning Goals + Hobbies side by side.
Grid → Best for 2D layouts (rows + columns). Example: full page layout with header, sidebar, main, footer.

Day 9 – Grid + Flexbox + Responsiveness Basics
Grid → Best for 2D layouts (rows + columns). Example: Goals + Hobbies.
display: grid;
grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
gap: 20px;
Flexbox → Best for 1D layouts (row OR column). Example: Education + Learning.
display: flex;
justify-content: space-between;
align-items: flex-start;
Combining Grid + Flexbox → Common in real websites (Grid for sections, Flexbox inside for alignment).
Gap → Built-in spacing in Grid/Flexbox. Better than margin.
Responsiveness → Start by making side-by-side content stack vertically on small screens.

Day 10 – Media Queries + Responsive Design
Responsive Design → Adjusts automatically for desktop, tablet, mobile.
Media Query Syntax →
@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .box { flex-direction: column; }
}
Breakpoints (common)
1200px → Large desktop
992px → Small laptop / landscape tablet
768px → Tablet portrait
576px → Mobile
Mobile-First Approach → Write default styles for mobile, then use min-width for larger screens.
Desktop-First Approach → Write default styles for desktop, then use max-width for smaller screens.
Testing Responsiveness → Shrink browser window or use Chrome DevTools (Ctrl+Shift+I → Toggle device toolbar).

Day 11 → Forms & Form Styling
Forms collect user input using <form>, sent via GET or POST.
Input attributes:
type → text, email, password, checkbox, radio, submit, etc.
name → key sent to the server.
id → used with <label> and CSS/JS.
value → default or submitted value.
placeholder → hint inside input.
required, disabled, readonly → control user input.
Label: for="id" links label to input (better accessibility).
Select & Textarea → dropdowns and multi-line input.
Styling Inputs:
Use input[type="text"] {} to style specific input types.
:focus → highlight active fields.
:checked → style checked checkboxes/radio.
input[type="submit"]:hover → add button hover effects.
Form UX: Rounded borders, focus effects, hover effects improve user experience.
Rule of thumb: Clean forms = more user-friendly websites.

Day 12 → Pseudo-classes & Pseudo-elements
Pseudo-classes = element state (special situations):
:hover → when mouse is over.
:focus → input active.
:checked → checkbox/radio selected.
:first-child, :last-child, :nth-child() → select specific elements.
Pseudo-elements = virtual elements (parts of an element):
::before → insert content before element.
::after → insert content after element.
::first-letter → style only first letter.
::first-line → style only first line.
Why important:
Add decorations without extra HTML.
Cleaner code (no <span> hacks).
Power up modern UI design (arrows, emojis, tooltips, highlights).
Rule of thumb:
Pseudo-classes = “when something happens”.
Pseudo-elements = “add something extra”.
